  <h2 id="postgres">The Postgres schema</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> set, every orca binary creates and migrates the
    <code>orca</code> schema at start-up. Each change of a run's state is one
    transaction. orca 0.23 has 54 migrations, and 0.24.0 adds a 55th
    (<code>orca.pipelines.fill_max_runs_auto</code>). A <code>sqlite:</code> database
    holds the core run tables only.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Table (orca.*)</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tables.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="migrations">Additive migrations</h3>
  <p>
    A binary older than the database runs on it if every migration it doesn't know is
    additive, which is what makes <code>deploy.sh --rollback</code> safe. So a migration
    may add tables, indexes and nullable foreign keys, and columns that are nullable or
    have a default. It may not rename, drop or retype a column or table, tighten a
    constraint, or write a value an older binary would act on. A migration that must is
    listed as raising the floor: <code>orca.schema_version</code> records it, and a binary
    that doesn't know it refuses to start. Today only migrations 1 and 24 raise it.
    Queries name the columns they read and write, so new columns never break old binaries.
    The rules are in <a href={`${SRC}/migrations.mojo`} rel="noopener">migrations.mojo</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="env">Environment variables</h2>
  <p>
    Settings for the whole stack are environment variables, in
    <code>/etc/orca.env</code> on a deployed stack; settings for one pipeline belong in
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/config#env">orca.toml's env</a>. Families with many members
    (<code>ORCA_WATCHDOG_*</code>, <code>ORCA_NOTIFY_*</code>, <code>ORCA_EC2_*</code>,
    <code>ORCA_POOL_*</code>) are listed in full in
    <a href={`${DOCS}/watchdog.md`} rel="noopener">watchdog.md</a>,
    <a href={`${DOCS}/alerts.md`} rel="noopener">alerts.md</a>,
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/run#env">Scheduling and running</a> and
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/config#pools">orca.toml</a>.
  </p>

